adjudication.rationale:
The new helper `parseRefreshTokenResponse` explicitly overwrites missing `refresh_token` with the literal string `"refresh_token"`:
```ts
if (!refreshTokenResponse.data.refresh_token) {
  refreshTokenResponse.data.refresh_token = "refresh_token";
}
```
In several integrations the parsed token data is then merged back into stored credentials and persisted. For example Office365 now does:
```ts
const tokenResponse = parseRefreshTokenResponse(responseJson, refreshTokenResponseSchema);
o365AuthCredentials = { ...o365AuthCredentials, ...(tokenResponse.success && tokenResponse.data) };
await prisma.credential.update({ data: { key: o365AuthCredentials } });
```
When `APP_CREDENTIAL_SHARING_ENABLED && CALCOM_CREDENTIAL_SYNC_ENDPOINT` is set, `parseRefreshTokenResponse` uses `minimumTokenResponseSchema` (which does not require a refresh token) and then injects the literal placeholder, which can overwrite a real stored refresh token on update. Zoom similarly replaces `safeParse` with `parseRefreshTokenResponse(responseBody, zoomRefreshedTokenSchema)`, which can propagate the placeholder into whatever persistence happens later in that flow. This is a real data-corruption risk introduced by the diff.
